WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.348
<v Jamie>Snowflake just surged thirty-five percent on AI hype—which is the Crocs IPO of corporate governance: completely unhinged, nobody knows why it works, and somehow it's printing money.

00:00:10.348 --> 00:00:31.045
<v Alex>S&P 500 up point five-two percent to seventy-five fifty-nine, Nasdaq climbing point six-five percent to twenty-six thousand eight hundred and forty-eight. V I X is down two points, so nobody's panicking, which is its own kind of panic if you think about it. The real story though—Oracle just ripped six point zero-one percent.

00:00:31.045 --> 00:00:39.860
<v Jamie>Oracle? Isn't that the database company that somehow became a cloud company that somehow became an AI company? How does that happen?

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:55.958
<v Alex>They hired a CEO with a printing press and a PowerPoint. But seriously—Therapeutics up five-point-two-three percent, A M D up four-point-six-nine, Lily up four-point-three-eight. This is a tech-weighted market right now. The Crocs IPO of corporate governance is still working.

00:00:55.958 --> 00:01:03.240
<v Maya>Wait, so Oracle's doing the same thing Snowflake did? Just saying "AI" and watching the stock go vertical?

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:17.805
<v Alex>Oracle's been saying "AI" since 2019. The difference is they actually have revenue. Snowflake has revenue too, but Oracle has, like, the kind of revenue that makes you feel like you're not insane for buying the stock.

00:01:17.805 --> 00:01:25.087
<v Jamie>So Oracle gets a pass because they have the receipts. What about the losers? Who got hurt today?

00:01:25.087 --> 00:01:36.586
<v Maya>Visa down one-point-six-four percent, Netflix down one-point-three-six, McDonald's down one-point-zero-five. These are like the blue-chip equivalent of a bad day at the office—nobody's bleeding out, they're just quietly disappointing.

00:01:36.586 --> 00:01:53.833
<v Alex>Okay, so Everpure beat earnings yesterday. Revenue up, profit margins expanded, guidance raised. Stock immediately dropped in after-hours. This is the inverse Snowflake. This is what happens when you do everything right and the market says, "Yeah, but did you make us *feel* something?"
  ↳ source: https://www.investors.com/news/technology/everpure-stock-earnings-2026-april-pure-storage/?src=A00220&yptr=yahoo

00:01:53.833 --> 00:02:06.098
<v Jamie>Wait, they beat and raised guidance and the stock got punished? That's not how this is supposed to work. What did the CEO say on the call that broke the vibe?

00:02:06.098 --> 00:02:17.213
<v Maya>Okay so the CEO said cautious things about consumer spending in Q2. She might as well have said, "We have no idea what's coming and we're terrified."

00:02:17.213 --> 00:02:41.743
<v Alex>The earnings were good. The guidance was good. But she whispered caution and the market heard, "We're about to crater." This is peak market psychology. The numbers are a formality now. It's all about the CEO's tone, the cadence, whether they sound like they actually believe their own forward guidance or if they're just reading it because their I R team made them.

00:02:41.743 --> 00:02:49.792
<v Jamie>So Everpure chose violence today by being honest about macro uncertainty instead of pretending everything's fine. That's the real crime.

00:02:49.792 --> 00:03:08.955
<v Maya>And now everyone's comparing Everpure to Snowflake like, "See? Snowflake didn't beat earnings but it sounded confident, so it went up thirty-five percent. Everpure beat earnings but sounded nervous, so it died." The stock market is literally a confidence game where the actual business results are just set dressing.
  ↳ source: https://www.investors.com/news/technology/everpure-stock-earnings-2026-april-pure-storage/?src=A00220&yptr=yahoo

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:38.468
<v Alex>Everpure has the receipts. They have the revenue, the margin expansion, the raised guidance. But the caution cost them in market cap. That's the Crocs IPO of corporate governance finally eating itself—when the vibe machine breaks down and realizes it was never actually about the fundamentals. Best Buy climbed fifteen percent on earnings—the one retailer that figured out people still want to touch a T V before buying it, which is apparently a competitive moat now.

00:03:38.468 --> 00:03:43.067
<v Jamie>Wait, that's the entire turnaround strategy? "Come in, feel the pixels"?

00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:57.248
<v Alex>Best Buy reported better-than-expected earnings and raised guidance. Sales are actually moving. Turns out when you're the only place left where you can walk in and not buy something online five minutes later, you have leverage.

00:03:57.248 --> 00:04:06.830
<v Maya>So Best Buy's competitive advantage is that it has a building and humans in it? That's not a moat, that's just real estate arbitrage.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:17.179
<v Alex>It's the Crocs IPO of retail strategy—completely backwards, shouldn't work, and yet here we are. Fifteen percent in one day because consumers remembered that showrooms exist.

00:04:17.179 --> 00:04:29.444
<v Jamie>Inflation just swamped income gains, the Fed's eyeing rate hikes, but the S&P 500 is still up—the market's commitment issues are showing. It knows the math is broken but keeps buying anyway.

00:04:29.444 --> 00:04:40.942
<v Maya>The actual numbers are grim though, right? Like, real wage growth is negative when you account for inflation, so everyone's getting poorer in real terms but stocks keep ripping.

00:04:40.942 --> 00:04:58.573
<v Alex>The math says people should be liquidating everything and buying canned beans. Instead, the S&P is up point five-two percent. The market is choosing to believe that either inflation breaks or the Fed pivots, and it's not doing the work to figure out which one.

00:04:58.573 --> 00:05:05.472
<v Jamie>So we're in the "hope is not a strategy but it's working" phase of the market cycle.

00:05:05.472 --> 00:05:20.036
<v Alex>Hope is literally the only strategy. The Fed is watching inflation overshoot their two percent target and the market is like, "Yeah but what if they don't actually raise rates because the stock market would be sad?"

00:05:20.036 --> 00:05:30.768
<v Maya>This is peak market psychology. Everpure said cautious things and lost market cap. But inflation is actually rising and everyone's like, "Nah, we're fine, buy the dip."

00:05:30.768 --> 00:05:43.033
<v Jamie>Iran targeted a U.S. base after fresh American strikes—escalation spiral is live, oil markets are pricing in "maybe nothing happens," which is either genius or the most dangerous assumption in the room.
  ↳ source: https://www.npr.org/2026/05/28/g-s1-124621/up-first-newsletter-iran-lebanon-israel-us-war-ebola-congo-food-insecurity-google-polymarket

00:05:43.033 --> 00:05:50.699
<v Alex>What's oil doing? Because if the market actually believed this was serious, crude would be north of eighty dollars.

00:05:50.699 --> 00:06:03.730
<v Jamie>So the market's basically saying, "Yeah, Iran fired on an American military installation, but we're pretty sure nobody's going to actually do anything about it." That's confidence or delusion, I haven't decided which.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:16.378
<v Maya>The hostilities are happening during a ceasefire negotiation, so it's like both sides are saying, "We're talking peace AND also we're shooting at each other." That's not a ceasefire, that's a performance.

00:06:16.378 --> 00:06:31.326
<v Alex>Oil markets pricing in "maybe nothing happens" is the market equivalent of cautious guidance—except this time it's about actual military escalation. If the market's wrong about the oil call, crude spikes and the entire inflation picture changes overnight.

00:06:31.326 --> 00:06:39.375
<v Jamie>So we're holding our breath on both the Fed and Iran while the S&P 500 pretends neither of those things matter.

00:06:39.375 --> 00:06:49.340
<v Maya>Roku's secret to success is not being cool—the anti-hype play that somehow became the streaming box that actually works, which is the most Roku thing ever.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/column/938879/roku-homescreen-redesign-not-cool

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:52.023
<v Jamie>Wait, Roku's strategy is "be boring"?

00:06:52.023 --> 00:07:11.187
<v Maya>Yeah, so Roku just quietly became the dominant streaming platform by not trying to be H B O Max or Netflix or Apple T V. They're the operating system underneath everything else. They don't make the content, they don't hype the content, they just... exist as the infrastructure.

00:07:11.187 --> 00:07:24.985
<v Alex>So Roku's competitive advantage is that it refused to play the hype game. Every other streaming company is trying to be the next Netflix. Roku said, "What if we're just the thing that runs Netflix?"

00:07:24.985 --> 00:07:34.950
<v Jamie>That's the inverse of Snowflake. Snowflake went up thirty-five percent on vibes and no revenue. Roku went up by not having vibes and just... working.

00:07:34.950 --> 00:08:03.313
<v Maya>Roku is what happens when a company decides to do the exact opposite—don't say anything interesting, don't promise anything exciting, just deliver the product and let the numbers talk. Boring is the new hype. Okay, so the DOJ just charged a Google staffer for allegedly using insider information to trade on Polymarket—this is a prediction market where you bet on outcomes, elections, sports, whatever. The charges include wire fraud and conspiracy.

00:08:03.313 --> 00:08:09.445
<v Jamie>Wait, so this guy had access to sensitive information and he just... bet on it?

00:08:09.445 --> 00:08:36.275
<v Maya>Exactly. And the timing is absolutely unhinged because while the DOJ is prosecuting him for allegedly knowing too much about future events, the U.S. is actively striking Iran, which is definitely affecting the prediction markets he was trading on. So the federal government is simultaneously bombing the Middle East AND investigating whether this one Google employee had an unfair information advantage on Polymarket. The regulatory priorities are truly cooked.

00:08:36.275 --> 00:09:14.602
<v Alex>The DOJ is charging a guy for insider trading on a prediction market while the actual inside information is being generated in real time by the U.S. military. This is the most American thing possible: we'll prosecute the individual for knowing too much, but the government gets to literally create the news and trade on it. Consumer Price Index drops Wednesday morning. Markets are pricing in a soft landing, but if headline inflation ticks up even point one percent, the Fed pivot story evaporates and we're back to "rates stay higher for longer." The bond market will immediately reprice.

00:09:14.602 --> 00:09:35.299
<v Maya>And then Wednesday night we've got Best Buy, Dollar Tree, Kohl's, Burlington, Build-A-Bear—basically the entire discount retail ecosystem reporting on the same day. If consumers are actually holding up like the market believes, these earnings will prove it. If they're not, we get the real story about whether people are actually broke.

00:09:35.299 --> 00:09:44.498
<v Jamie>So Best Buy's already up fifteen percent on showroom vibes. What happens if they miss on the next quarter and the momentum dies?

00:09:44.498 --> 00:10:04.429
<v Alex>Best Buy becomes the Crocs IPO that actually was a Crocs IPO—a one-day pop that the market immediately regrets. If they guide down, that fifteen percent evaporates and we're back to asking why anyone shops there in person when they can just order from Amazon and return it to Whole Foods.

00:10:04.429 --> 00:10:18.610
<v Maya>Dollar Tree and Kohl's are the real tells though. If those numbers are weak, it means even the discount customer is tapped out. That's the market's "hope is not a strategy" moment finally arriving with receipts.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:35.857
<v Alex>Snowflake went up thirty-five percent on the promise of AI and is now trading like it invented the cloud instead of just renting space in it—this is what happens when a company's valuation becomes a hostage negotiation between the stock price and actual earnings.

00:10:35.857 --> 00:10:46.206
<v Maya>Meanwhile Roku's sitting there with zero AI announcements and genuine revenue, watching Snowflake's chart and thinking, "We could do that, but we'd have to lie first."

00:10:46.206 --> 00:10:51.572
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
